  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 AM EST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nTrof-west, ridge-east upper pattern, at least at the moment. Things \nare becoming more progressive, and though the upper trof will \ninitially minor out somewhat in the central states, reinforcements \nfrom the northern stream will reinvigorate the trof in the Sun-Mon \ntimeframe. This drives a cold front across the area later Sunday, \nwith much cooler wx next week.\n\nDeep moisture remains quite unimpressive until quiet close to the \nsurface cold front. This will edge toward western Chip/Mack Cos in \neastern upper MI late today into tonight. Otherwise quiet today. \nCirrus will decrease in the early AM, with mostly sunny skies for \nmost of the area today, except for some increasing clouds late in \nthe west and north. Temps will become quite warm. All of todays\nrecord highs are in at least a little bit of danger:\n\n ANJ 63 (1913)\n PLN 59 (1990) (Note that PLN is 58f at 1 am)\n GLR 62 (1953)\n APN 65 (1931)\n TVC 68 (1913)\n HTL 64 (1913) \n\nModels are continuing to slightly slow cold fropa Sunday, which will \nallow that day to also be quite warm. We don't threaten as many \nrecords, but we might approach some in and adjacent to ne lower MI \n(APN/GLR/HTL). \n\nBand of showers still looks likely to accompany cold front across \nnorthern MI, with a period of likely pops or higher in the west and \nnorth , as sharp heights falls commence. Band is likely to start to \nbecome disorganized as it crosses the area, and pops decrease \nsomewhat over there. QPF will be around 0.25\" locally. This period \nof synoptic precip will be all liquid. However, 850mb temps do \ntumble to slightly below 0C post-frontal Sunday evening, and showers \nwill get going in nw-flow lake effect belts. These will initially be \nliquid, but as still colder air bleeds in, leftover lake effect will \nchange to snow late Sunday night. Though instability will be \nabundant, the airmass is initially dry, and lake effect coverage/ \nintensity limited. An uptick in activity is expected Monday \nafternoon and early evening, as a reinforcing shortwave moves thru. \nThat's an unfavorable time of day to get snow to stick. A coating of \nsnow could be temporarily laid down in favored nw flow locales, \nespecially Monday evening. \n\nAfter that, high pressure builds eastward from the upper MS Valley \nacross the central lakes, drying us out further. East coast \ncyclogenesis will occur mid and late week, though impacts here for \nthe holiday period will be minimal. Perhaps some spotty light precip \npossible on Thanksgiving Day, but nothing substantial.\n\nWarm up thru ahead of fropa Sunday, sharply cooler rest of the week, \nwith highs mid 30s to mid 40s Monday to Friday.",
